# 04 — Orienting Comments

Every new or updated field, property, constructor, function, method, and enum member needs an orienting comment. This covers static and instance members, top-level and nested declarations, and every access level, including exported, non-exported, and private declarations. Only members marked with the `override` keyword are exempt.

## Format

In TypeScript, write one JSDoc `/** ... */` block directly above the declaration. `npm run lint` rejects blocks under `src/` that do not use this layout, which `.spec/completed/2026-04/2026-04-10-orienting-comments-style-contract-v1.md` describes in full:

1. A one-line summary with no label.
2. `Purpose:` why the declaration exists. Interface members and abstract methods use `Contract:` instead, stating what callers can rely on.
3. `When to use:` when and how to use it.
4. `Expected outcome:` what it returns or produces.
5. `Exceptions:` which errors it can throw, or `None.`

For concrete functions and methods, summarize how they work at a high level.

## Content

Write specific text for each declaration. Do not use generic filler such as "Use per team conventions." Existing filler comments may stay until you update their declarations; then replace them with specific text.
